## 2. Data Source

Posts live as `.md` / `.mdx` files in `src/content/blogs/`, either flat or in
topic subfolders (e.g. `src/content/blogs/skin-tone/best-nude-nail-color-for-skin-tone.md`).

- `.md` → compiled to HTML at request time via `marked` (in `blog.ts`)
- `.mdx` → raw source passed to `<MDXRemote>` (rendered via `MDXContent.tsx`)

Full field-by-field breakdown of frontmatter is in **§14 Frontmatter Fields
Reference** — read that before adding/editing any post.

`template` value picks the render template: `center | split-left | split-right | grid`.

### topicalMap and slug — the two fields that matter most

These two drive routing, related-posts logic, and hub-page behavior. Get
these wrong and posts either 404, land in the wrong place, or don't show up
where expected.

**`slug`** — not actually a frontmatter field; it's derived from the
**filename** (minus extension), via `path.basename()`. So a file at
`src/content/blogs/skin-tone/best-nude-nail-color.md` has slug
`best-nude-nail-color` regardless of which subfolder it's in.
`resolveFilePath()` in `blog.ts` searches recursively and matches on this
bare filename (or full relative path) — meaning **filenames must be unique
across the entire `blogs/` tree**, not just within a subfolder. Two files
named the same thing in different folders will collide.

**`topicalMap`** — controls how a post relates to other posts:
```
topicalMap: {
  position: 'pillar' | 'cluster' | 'standalone',
  parentPillar: string | null   // pillar's slug, for cluster posts only
}
```
- A **pillar** post + its **cluster** posts (linked via `parentPillar`) form
  a "topic family."
- Visiting `/blog/[pillarSlug]` (1 URL segment) renders the **Topic Hub**
  (`PillarHub` component) instead of the pillar's own content — a listing
  page of the pillar + all its clusters. The pillar's actual article only
  renders at `/blog/[topic]/[pillarSlug]` (2 segments).
- `getAllClusters()`, `getStandalonePosts()`, `getRelatedPosts()`, and the
  `/blog` listing pagination (`buildPageAllocation`) all key off this field.
  Getting `position`/`parentPillar` wrong on a post silently breaks its
  related-posts and homepage-carousel placement — no error is thrown.

